WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.110
<v Maya>Welcome to part six.

2
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.980
<v Maya>We have finished body composition, and DEXA came out as the most recommended.

3
00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:14.650
<v Maya>Today is test three in the order, the cardiorespiratory fitness tests, also called cardiorespiratory endurance tests.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.240
<v Dev>Two types, right?

5
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.130
<v Maya>Yes.

6
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.700
<v Maya>The VO₂ max test and the heart rate recovery test.

7
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:29.580
<v Maya>The slides define VO₂ max as an individual's aerobic capacity, the maximal oxygen uptake during incremental exercise.

8
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:36.420
<v Maya>Heart rate recovery is the ability of the heart to return towards normal heart rate within a specific time after activity.

9
00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.310
<v Maya>Recall the cardio lecture.

10
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:49.320
<v Maya>In an incremental activity, brisk walk, then jog, then run, then sprint, heart rate rises with intensity, and so does VO₂, the volume of oxygen consumed to make energy.

11
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:58.670
<v Maya>At the point where heart rate cannot rise any more, near maximum heart rate, the oxygen consumed is the VO₂ max, the maximum aerobic capacity.

12
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.690
<v Dev>So higher VO₂ max means better cardio fitness.

13
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:03.580
<v Maya>Yes.

14
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.840
<v Maya>The test has two sub categories.

15
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.610
<v Maya>Maximal, for athletes.

16
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.700
<v Maya>The person is taken all the way to their peak, above eighty five percent, the sprinting zone.

17
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:19.470
<v Maya>Sub-maximal, for general lifestyle clients, because not every client can or should be taken to maximum effort.

18
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.780
<v Maya>Most clients are business people, homemakers, students and office workers, and they deserve to know their VO₂ max too.

19
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.390
<v Dev>What does the maximal test look like?

20
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:38.640
<v Maya>In a laboratory, an athlete does an incremental activity, for example a treadmill whose incline increases every minute, aiming for a peak in about ten minutes.

21
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:47.400
<v Maya>They are connected by tubes to a system that measures breathing, oxygen consumed and carbon dioxide produced, in real time, every second.

22
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:53.410
<v Maya>The screen shows a split graph, heart rate rising and VO₂ rising, in millilitres per kilogram.

23
00:01:53.460 --> 00:02:01.230
<v Maya>The video also said it estimates the lactate threshold so training zones can be set, and it is best used to see change over time.

24
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.490
<v Dev>So that's for athletes in a lab.

25
00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.180
<v Dev>What do I use with a normal client?

26
00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.980
<v Maya>The Rockport one mile walking fitness test.

27
00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:14.090
<v Maya>Walk one mile, which is one point six kilometres, as fast as possible, non-stop.

28
00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.590
<v Maya>You can measure it with a vehicle trip meter.

29
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.280
<v Maya>And it has to be as fast as possible, even though it is sub-maximal, because it still estimates VO₂ max.

30
00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.600
<v Maya>Not a relaxed post dinner stroll.

31
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.860
<v Dev>How do we turn a walk into a VO₂ max?

32
00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.850
<v Maya>A formula with four inputs.

33
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.080
<v Maya>Time in minutes.

34
00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.030
<v Maya>Pulse.

35
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:33.930
<v Maya>Age.

36
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.330
<v Maya>And weight in pounds, not kilograms.

37
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.090
<v Maya>Multiply kilograms by two point two.

38
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.860
<v Maya>Sixty kilograms times two point two is one hundred and thirty two pounds.

39
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:45.770
<v Dev>How do you take the pulse?

40
00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.710
<v Maya>Immediately at the end, for ten seconds, then multiply by six.

41
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.360
<v Maya>Say you count eighteen beats in ten seconds.

42
00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.910
<v Maya>Eighteen times six is one hundred and eight beats per minute.

43
00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.570
<v Maya>Why not a full minute?

44
00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.920
<v Maya>Because the heart rate would recover during that minute, and this test needs the peak.

45
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.260
<v Maya>The first ten seconds are the highest.

46
00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.910
<v Dev>But VO₂ max is always a one minute volume.

47
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.890
<v Dev>Why accept an aggregate?

48
00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.380
<v Maya>In the lab, the machine measures oxygen live, so it gives a per minute figure.

49
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:19.250
<v Maya>Here you finish the activity first and note the pulse after, so there's no live measurement.

50
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.000
<v Maya>Ten seconds times six stands in for the minute.

51
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.250
<v Maya>The formulas.

52
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:40.160
<v Maya>For women, VO₂ max equals one hundred thirty two point eight five, minus zero point three eight eight times age, minus zero point zero seven seven times weight in pounds, minus three point two six five times time, minus zero point one five six times pulse.

53
00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:44.630
<v Maya>For men, the same, but starting at one hundred thirty nine point one six eight.

54
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.690
<v Maya>Only that first number differs.

55
00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.360
<v Maya>You don't have to memorise them, online calculators ask for the same four inputs.

56
00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.850
<v Maya>The result is in millilitres of oxygen per kilogram of body weight per minute.

57
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.200
<v Maya>Case one, male.

58
00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:02.130
<v Maya>Age fifty, weight sixty kilograms, time fifteen minutes, pulse one hundred and eight.

59
00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.700
<v Maya>Weight is one hundred and thirty two pounds.

60
00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.000
<v Maya>Now each term.

61
00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:09.990
<v Maya>Zero point three eight eight times fifty is nineteen point four.

62
00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.300
<v Maya>Zero point zero seven seven times one thirty two is ten point one six four.

63
00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:20.050
<v Maya>Three point two six five times fifteen is forty eight point nine seven five.

64
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.490
<v Maya>Zero point one five six times one hundred and eight is sixteen point eight four eight.

65
00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:26.920
<v Dev>Let me add them.

66
00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.880
<v Dev>Nineteen point four plus ten point one six four is twenty nine point five six four.

67
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:37.920
<v Dev>Plus forty eight point nine seven five is seventy eight point five three nine.

68
00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:42.570
<v Dev>Plus sixteen point eight four eight is ninety five point three eight seven.

69
00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:43.560
<v Maya>Correct.

70
00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.560
<v Maya>Subtract from one thirty nine point one six eight.

71
00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.970
<v Maya>That gives forty three point seven eight one.

72
00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.710
<v Maya>The slide prints forty three point seven nine, so check your slide.

73
00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.760
<v Maya>Same category either way.

74
00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.030
<v Dev>Which is?

75
00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.380
<v Maya>Go to the chart.

76
00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.360
<v Maya>It is looked up by gender, age and the result.

77
00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:09.980
<v Maya>For males at age fifty, Superior is fifty and above, Excellent is forty nine to forty five, Very good is forty four to forty, Good is thirty nine to thirty five.

78
00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:13.350
<v Maya>Forty three point seven nine falls in Very good.

79
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.860
<v Dev>How do I read the chart in general?

80
00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:22.700
<v Maya>Row is gender and age, the columns are the fitness levels from Superior down to Very poor, and higher is better.

81
00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:27.990
<v Maya>The chart lists ages in steps of five, so it doesn't say what to do for ages in between.

82
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.670
<v Maya>Check your course material.

83
00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.220
<v Maya>Case two, the female case from the slide, which wasn't discussed in the lecture.

84
00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:40.320
<v Maya>Age thirty eight, weight one hundred and forty pounds, time twenty one minutes, pulse one hundred and forty six.

85
00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:45.040
<v Maya>Zero point three eight eight times thirty eight is fourteen point seven four four.

86
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.880
<v Maya>Zero point zero seven seven times one forty is ten point seven eight.

87
00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:54.740
<v Maya>Three point two six five times twenty one is sixty eight point five six five.

88
00:05:54.790 --> 00:06:00.410
<v Maya>Zero point one five six times one forty six is twenty two point seven seven six.

89
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.870
<v Maya>These add up to one hundred sixteen point eight six five.

90
00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:08.390
<v Maya>One thirty two point eight five minus that is fifteen point nine eight five.

91
00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:09.640
<v Maya>Very poor.

92
00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:12.410
<v Dev>That's a very slow walk and high pulse.

93
00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:13.300
<v Maya>Yes.

94
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.510
<v Maya>Pause and think.

95
00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.110
<v Maya>Why does a client's weight need to be in pounds for this formula?

96
00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.630
<v Dev>Because the formula asks for pounds.

97
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.140
<v Dev>If I put in kilograms, the answer comes out wrong.

98
00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:25.320
<v Maya>Exactly.

99
00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.270
<v Maya>Recap.

100
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:35.120
<v Maya>Rockport: one mile as fast as possible, four inputs, pulse ten seconds times six, VO₂ max looked up in a chart by gender and age.

101
00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:40.410
<v Maya>For your course note that the E-book says brisk pace, almost as fast as possible without jogging.

102
00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.210
<v Maya>Now heart rate recovery.

103
00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:47.630
<v Maya>The client does an activity, heart rate rises, and we check how well and how soon it recovers.

104
00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.610
<v Maya>Dev, who has better cardio fitness, the person whose heart takes more time to recover, or less?

105
00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.000
<v Dev>Less time.

106
00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.550
<v Maya>Yes, a faster recovery means a healthy and fit heart.

107
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:06.160
<v Maya>The test is the YMCA, the Young Men's Christian Association, a well known international body, three minute step test.

108
00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:10.390
<v Maya>For three minutes, non-stop, the client steps up and down on a step board.

109
00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.690
<v Dev>Why are the details so fixed?

110
00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.070
<v Maya>Standardisation.

111
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:20.260
<v Maya>Every step must be done in exactly the same way, otherwise results are not comparable and can't be looked up.

112
00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.730
<v Maya>A modified test gives a number that isn't worth looking up.

113
00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.470
<v Maya>So the YMCA presets two conditions.

114
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.930
<v Maya>Board height, twelve inches, one foot.

115
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:32.810
<v Maya>And speed, ninety six beats per minute, set with a metronome.

116
00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:34.340
<v Dev>Metronome?

117
00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:38.180
<v Maya>A small device, used by drummers, that ticks at set beats per minute.

118
00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:43.720
<v Maya>Thirty would be too slow, two hundred would make clients fall, so after testing, ninety six was set.

119
00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:46.010
<v Maya>One step is up, up, down, down.

120
00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.080
<v Maya>A single tick for a single move.

121
00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:51.520
<v Maya>Tick one up, tick two up, tick three down, tick four down.

122
00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:53.730
<v Maya>The client just follows the beat.

123
00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.060
<v Dev>Procedure?

124
00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.030
<v Maya>Set the board and metronome.

125
00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.550
<v Maya>Step for three minutes.

126
00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.450
<v Maya>When time is over, the client sits down immediately.

127
00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.880
<v Maya>Then count the pulse for one complete minute.

128
00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.000
<v Maya>That is the recovery heart rate.

129
00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:09.070
<v Maya>Then look it up in the chart for gender and age.

130
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.150
<v Dev>Why a full minute here, but ten seconds times six in Rockport?

131
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.260
<v Maya>Here the purpose is recovery, so you need the heart time to recover, and you count the whole minute.

132
00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.210
<v Maya>In Rockport you needed the peak.

133
00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:24.100
<v Maya>Recovery needs the full minute, peak needs the first ten seconds.

134
00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.010
<v Dev>What does the trainer do?

135
00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:35.340
<v Maya>Gives rhythm cues, up, up, down, down, motivates, corrects the foot, whole foot on the board, calls out only thirty seconds remaining, counts down from ten.

136
00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.870
<v Maya>Then notes the pulse while watching the clock.

137
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.790
<v Maya>It takes presence of mind.

138
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:41.140
<v Dev>Case study?

139
00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:44.700
<v Maya>A female, thirty years, pulse eighty five after the test.

140
00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.020
<v Maya>Use the women's chart, twenty six to thirty five column.

141
00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.100
<v Maya>Excellent is below eighty eight.

142
00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:53.090
<v Maya>Eighty five is below eighty eight, so excellent.

143
00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:54.560
<v Maya>Lower is better here.

144
00:08:54.610 --> 00:09:00.290
<v Maya>The women's chart goes on to good at eighty eight to ninety nine, and very poor above one thirty eight.

145
00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.740
<v Dev>Men and women have separate charts.

146
00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:03.630
<v Maya>Yes.

147
00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.250
<v Maya>Quick table: Rockport needs peak heart rate, pulse ten seconds times six, no metronome.

148
00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:12.900
<v Maya>Step test needs recovery, full minute, metronome ninety six.

149
00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:14.070
<v Maya>Five things.

150
00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:21.680
<v Maya>One, VO₂ max is maximum oxygen consumption, maximal tests for athletes, sub-maximal for general clients.

151
00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:27.540
<v Maya>Two, Rockport is one mile as fast as possible, with time, pulse, age and weight in pounds.

152
00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:32.770
<v Maya>Three, pulse is ten seconds times six in Rockport, but a full minute in the step test.

153
00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.550
<v Maya>Four, the male example comes to forty three point seven eight one, Very good.

154
00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:45.150
<v Maya>Five, the YMCA test uses a twelve inch board, ninety six beats per minute, three minutes, and a faster recovery is better.

155
00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.730
<v Dev>Next, strength and endurance tests.

156
00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.090
<v Maya>See you in part seven.

